  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Business Botswana (BB) developed a study of the business environment, specifically the trade-licensing regime for business by investigating the prevalence of extra requirements imposed by local authorities. The study aimed at assessing how to reduce turnaround times, reduce the cost of doing business and increase transparency in registering and licensing businesses. BB validated the findings of the study at a virtual workshop that was held in June 2021 attended by 35 participants (65%women) representing Government and Business. Critically, BB used the results of the survey to advocate and recommend ways to ease stringent requirements for the registration of businesses, in particular, to remove subjectivity in the granting of licenses while ensuring predictability of rules for investors. 
In addition, based on the findings of the study, BB engaged with the tripartite Sector Level Consultative Council (SLCC) in September 2021. BB conducted a subsequent follow-up engagement at the High Level Consultative Council (HLCC) in November 2021. The HLCC is the highest level of engagement in the country where Business meets with His Excellency the President, together with cabinet ministers twice a year to discuss issues of national economic interest.
As a result of BB&#x2019;s policy advocacy, the Department of Trade and Consumer Affairs in the Ministry of Investment, Trade &amp; Industry briefed all Local Authorities on changes and resultant statutory requirements of the Trade Act of 2019 and the Regulations of 2020  to resolve the problem of inconsistent implementation of the Act. The Ministry made assurances that the online business licensing system will be linked and accessible to all Local Authorities and other Government Ministries and public procurement entities, and committed to purchasing new equipment for all Local Authorities to enable them to improve their service delivery to applicants for business licences and registration certificates. The ILO developed the scope of work for the study, provided technical guidance on the methodology of the study itself, and reviewed the report before it was presented at a validation workshop in June 2021. The review was conducted through structured interviews of Local Authorities, trade licence applicants or their agents as well as BB members in all regions in Botswana. The ILO provided coordination support towards organizing the validation of the report where in more inputs from the enterprises were received.  The ILO provided support by way of quality oversight in the formulation of the final report.  Importantly, the ILO built the capacity of BB by working closely with the nominated staff of BB to analyse the results of the study and, guide on how to use the same to develop a policy position for evidence-based advocacy. Through this process, the ILO guided BB on how to communicate the results of the study to its wider membership via a recently revamped website. This was made possible by the work during the biennium where, as part of the COVID-19 response, the ILO supported BB with the, design, branding and marketing of its website, which not only promoted BB&#x2019;s online presence, but also acted as a gateway to its services and policy positions.  
This support has enhanced BB&#x2019;s national presence, its brand equity and advocacy capacity.</narrative>
   </description>
